---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { loadPublicData } from '../lib/data';
import { vocabularyLabel } from '../lib/filter';

const data = loadPublicData();
const unassignedConcepts = data.documents.filter((document) => document.concepts.length === 0).length;
const unassignedPolicies = data.documents.filter((document) => document.policies.length === 0).length;
const linkedDocuments = new Set(data.relationships.flatMap((relationship) => [
  ...(relationship.source_entity_type === 'document' ? [relationship.source_entity_id] : []),
  ...(relationship.target_entity_type === 'document' ? [relationship.target_entity_id] : []),
]));
const unlinkedDocuments = data.documents.filter((document) => !linkedDocuments.has(document.id)).length;
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Methodology | EU AI Policy Observatory"
  description="Methodology for the published EU AI policy research corpus."
  canonicalPath="/methodology/"
>
  <article class="editorial-page">
    <header class="editorial-page__header">
      <p class="section-kicker">Methodology</p>
      <h1>Building a traceable policy corpus</h1>
      <p>
        The Observatory treats the database as research infrastructure and the website as a
        readable, static view of published records.
      </p>
    </header>

    <section aria-labelledby="current-method">
      <h2 id="current-method">Current corpus method</h2>
      <p>
        The published records now include a bounded, verified historical-lineage collection alongside
        contemporary EU AI policy and AI Act implementation material. Their document dates span
        {data.coverage.from_year}–{data.coverage.to_year},
        with a publication cutoff of {data.coverage.coverage_cutoff}.
        The approved research boundary also admits earlier European Communities and EU material,
        but historical backfill and the wider institutional and sectoral sweep remain incomplete.
        Formally published drafts can qualify; all additions still require record-level official-source verification.
        The research boundary concerns AI-related material, not all EU digital law.
        The current corpus represents English-language manifestations; it is not a completed multilingual search.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section
      id="methodology-coverage"
      aria-labelledby="coverage-and-audit"
      data-coverage-statement={data.coverage.coverage_statement}
      data-unresolved-candidates={data.coverage.unresolved_candidates}
    >
      <h2 id="coverage-and-audit">Coverage and audit status</h2>
      <p>{data.coverage.coverage_statement}</p>
      <dl>
        <dt>Publication cutoff</dt><dd>{data.coverage.coverage_cutoff}</dd>
        <dt>Registered source families</dt><dd>{data.coverage.source_families.total}</dd>
        <dt>Reviewed registered families</dt><dd>{data.coverage.source_families.by_status.reviewed}</dd>
        <dt>Not started</dt><dd>{data.coverage.source_families.by_status.not_started}</dd>
        <dt>In progress</dt><dd>{data.coverage.source_families.by_status.in_progress}</dd>
        <dt>Known gaps</dt><dd>{data.coverage.source_families.by_status.gap_found}</dd>
        <dt>Recheck due</dt><dd>{data.coverage.source_families.by_status.recheck_due}</dd>
        <dt>Included candidates</dt><dd>{data.coverage.inventory.included}</dd>
        <dt>Merged candidates</dt><dd>{data.coverage.inventory.merged}</dd>
        <dt>Excluded candidates</dt><dd>{data.coverage.inventory.excluded}</dd>
        <dt>Unresolved candidates</dt><dd>{data.coverage.unresolved_candidates}</dd>
      </dl>
      <p>These are aggregate states of registered, bounded searches, not a complete source matrix. Unregistered sources and unreviewed periods are not covered by these counts.</p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="expanded-evidence-review">
      <h2 id="expanded-evidence-review">Expanded evidence review</h2>
      <p>
        {data.coverage.historical_review.verified} published records have completed the expanded date,
        attribution and historical-classification evidence review. {data.coverage.historical_review.legacy_review_pending}
        retained records remain published under their existing checks while that additional evidence review is pending.
        Pending status is not a finding that an official record is false, and no temporal or relevance class is inferred for it.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="review-queues" aria-labelledby="review-queues-heading">
      <h2 id="review-queues-heading">Separate review queues</h2>
      <dl>
        <dt>Unpublished candidates awaiting a decision</dt><dd>{data.coverage.unresolved_candidates}</dd>
        <dt>Published records awaiting expanded evidence review</dt><dd>{data.coverage.historical_review.legacy_review_pending}</dd>
      </dl>
      <p>
        These are different populations. Candidates are not in the public document corpus;
        retained published records carry explicit evidence qualifications. Neither count measures
        all documents still undiscovered. Completed expanded review means the required evidence
        fields passed review, not exhaustive factual accuracy or independent human double-review.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="annotation-coverage" aria-labelledby="annotation-coverage-heading">
      <h2 id="annotation-coverage-heading">Annotation coverage and limits</h2>
      <p>These counts cover all {data.documents.length} published records, independently of Corpus filters.</p>
      <dl>
        <dt>Without assigned research concepts</dt><dd>{unassignedConcepts}</dd>
        <dt>Without assigned policy groupings</dt><dd>{unassignedPolicies}</dd>
        <dt>Without a recorded relationship endpoint</dt><dd>{unlinkedDocuments}</dd>
      </dl>
      <p>
        An empty annotation list is not evidence of absence. It does not distinguish unreviewed,
        inapplicable and reviewed-negative findings. Concept labels are browsing lenses, not a completed
        passage-level coding study. Sector tags record positive assignments: a cross-sector tag does
        not automatically match every sector filter. The map contains recorded relationship endpoints
        only; a missing node or link is not evidence of policy isolation.
      </p>
      <p>
        Before using these fields as research variables, define the analytical sample, coding units,
        inclusion and exclusion examples, missing-value treatment, version handling and review protocol.
        Counts of principal works, supporting records, versions and attachments are not interchangeable
        with counts of unique laws or independent analytical observations.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="review-attribution" aria-labelledby="review-attribution-heading">
      <h2 id="review-attribution-heading">Review credit and evidence-review actions</h2>
      <p>
        Public review credit identifies Yichen Hao as the project reviewer. The evidence-review date
        comes from the recorded assessment; its actor remains in canonical and downloadable data.
        This display is not a separately timestamped personal sign-off. Human review or release approval
        must be recorded with its actual scope and date; no historical actor or date is replaced by display credit.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="bounded-source-scopes">
      <h2 id="bounded-source-scopes">Bounded source scopes</h2>
      <p>
        Each entry records an actual bounded scan, not comprehensive EU coverage. An in-progress scope is partial;
        empty document-type or sector fields are shown as unspecified. A zero pending count means no candidate is
        pending within that admitted-set scan, not that no historical leads remain.
      </p>
      <p>
        A reproducible search retains its actual query or navigation sequence, filters, result list,
        pagination or stopping rule, timestamp, access limitations and candidate decisions.
        Some legacy search narratives lack those details; they are not fully reproducible searches,
        and missing historical evidence is not reconstructed as if it had been recorded at the time.
      </p>
      <ol>
        {data.coverage.source_scopes.map((scope) => (
          <li>
            <h3>{scope.name}</h3>
            <dl>
              <dt>Source family</dt><dd>{scope.source_family}</dd>
              <dt>Institution</dt><dd>{scope.institution}</dd>
              <dt>Status</dt><dd>{scope.scan_status === 'in_progress' ? 'Partial · in progress' : vocabularyLabel(scope.scan_status)}</dd>
              <dt>Covered interval</dt><dd>{scope.covered_from}–{scope.covered_through}</dd>
              <dt>Coverage cutoff</dt><dd>{scope.coverage_cutoff}</dd>
              <dt>Document types</dt><dd>{scope.covered_document_types.length === 0 ? 'Unspecified' : scope.covered_document_types.map(vocabularyLabel).join(', ')}</dd>
              <dt>Sectors</dt><dd>{scope.covered_sector_tags.length === 0 ? 'Unspecified' : scope.covered_sector_tags.map(vocabularyLabel).join(', ')}</dd>
              <dt>Pending candidates in scope</dt><dd>{scope.pending_candidate_count}</dd>
            </dl>
          </li>
        ))}
      </ol>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="corpus-tiers">
      <h2 id="corpus-tiers">Corpus tiers</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>Core</strong> records are central to a defined research pathway or lineage. <strong>Directly related</strong>
        records materially inform a selected policy pathway or documented historical development. <strong>Contextual</strong>
        records supply necessary surrounding policy or legal context. These tiers do not by themselves make a historical
        record an AI Act predecessor or member; policy memberships and relationships are modelled separately.
        <em>Excluded</em> is an inventory decision, not a published corpus tier.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="inventory-decisions">
      <h2 id="inventory-decisions">Inventory decisions</h2>
      <p>
        Every discovered candidate receives a reasoned decision: <code>included</code> creates or
        identifies a canonical document record; <code>merged</code> links another manifestation to
        an existing record; <code>excluded</code> records a verified boundary failure; and
        <code>pending</code> retains unresolved official availability, identity or metadata for later
        review. Pending candidates are counted in aggregate audit summaries but are excluded from
        public document records, published record counts and downloads.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="publication-workflow">
      <h2 id="publication-workflow">Publication workflow</h2>
      <p><code>draft → pending_review → verified → published</code></p>
      <p>
        Records move through these stages as their evidence and editorial review are completed.
        Only published records appear in the public interface.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="evidence-and-analysis">
      <h2 id="evidence-and-analysis">Official evidence and analytical judgement</h2>
      <p>
        Official metadata, sources and stated relationships are kept distinct from
        researcher-authored corpus assessments, concepts and analytical relationships. This
        distinction makes clear whether a statement originates with an EU institution or with
        the research classification. An analytical relationship carries a researcher-written
        rationale and an evidence source; that source supports the assessment without turning it
        into an official EU relationship.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="seed-and-analysis-samples">
      <h2 id="seed-and-analysis-samples">Database seed and analytical samples</h2>
      <p>
        The seven-record original database seed remains a versioned operational subset. It is not the frozen
        all-route baseline, the full published corpus, or a researcher-approved PhD analytical sample. Any PhD
        sample will be selected and documented through a separate research protocol.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="versions-and-dates">
      <h2 id="versions-and-dates">Versions and date semantics</h2>
      <p>
        A principal document is distinct from supporting files, formal versions and separately
        citable attachments. Version relationships preserve the sequence between drafts, revisions,
        final and consolidated texts without treating each as a new policy. <code>document_date</code>
        dates the particular text or version; <code>publication_date</code> records its official
        publication or release date; and <code>event_date</code> dates a policy event. The coverage
        range is derived from published documents’ <code>document_date</code> values.
      </p>
      <p>
        For a retained record with an explicit pending-review qualification, an unconfirmed
        publication date is stored as null and displayed as “Publication date not yet confirmed”.
        It is excluded from publication-year filters and sorted after dated records; an adoption
        or retrieval date is never substituted. The previous value and correction reason remain
        in the audit history. Fully verified records still require exact dates and supporting evidence.
      </p>
      <p>
        Qualified review notes distinguish confirmed original text and publication from missing
        parent-approval evidence, an unconfirmed public-release date, or conflicting official version
        information. These notes do not promote a record to completed expanded verification.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="provenance">
      <h2 id="provenance">Provenance</h2>
      <p>
        Each published record retains its source links, retrieval and verification information,
        so that readers can trace displayed claims to their stated evidence.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="future-research">
      <h2 id="future-research">Planned LLM comparison protocol</h2>
      <p>
        A future study will compare large language model interpretations against selected official
        texts using a separately documented sampling, prompting and coding protocol. No model
        outputs, experiments or comparison results are implemented in this release; the method
        described above concerns corpus construction and publication only.
      </p>
    </section>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
